/* ==============================================================
   05 — Turn configuration: the turning shaft
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetConfTurn, SetAutoConfTurn, and joint 4
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted. A strip of tape on
             the shaft makes joint 4 much easier to watch.
   WATCH     Joint 4 reaching the same angle by two routes
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   JOINT 4 IS DIFFERENT

   Joints 1, 2 and 3 have mechanical limits. Joint 4 — the shaft
   turning about its own axis — does not. It can keep turning,
   and its software limit is plus or minus ten full turns.

   That creates an ambiguity the other joints do not have. If you
   ask for a tool orientation, joint 4 at 10 degrees and joint 4
   at 370 degrees produce exactly the same orientation. The tool
   looks identical. The shaft has turned a full extra revolution.

   The TURN CONFIGURATION parameter, ct, says which revolution
   you meant:

       SetConfTurn(ct)      ct an integer from -10 to 10

   It selects the range for joint 4 as
   -180 + ct*360 < θ4 <= 180 + ct*360. So ct = 0 is the normal
   range, ct = 1 is one full turn further on, and so on.

   WHY ANYONE CARES: WHATEVER IS CONNECTED TO THE SHAFT

   For a bare shaft, none of this matters and you should leave it
   alone. It matters when something is CONNECTED to the tool on
   the shaft — a powered gripper's cable, a sensor lead, an air
   or vacuum tube. On a SCARA that is the common case: the tool
   turns with joint 4, and whatever feeds it has to follow.

   Cable routing at 5 degrees and at 365 degrees is not the same
   thing at all. One is slack; the other has a full wrap round
   the shaft. Do that a few thousand times in one direction and
   the cable fails, or the tube kinks and pulls off its fitting.
   Wear at the tool is one of the most common causes of
   unplanned downtime on any robot, and it is entirely avoidable.

   The recommended treatment for anything connected to the shaft:
     1. Restrict joint 4 with SetJointLimits to the range the
        cable or tube can actually tolerate.
     2. Specify the turn with SetConfTurn so the robot always
        arrives at a pose the same way round.

   For a tool with nothing connected, leave automatic turn
   selection on. The manual is explicit about this: with a
   cable-less end-effector, automatic turn configuration should
   never be disabled.

   ONE PRACTICAL WARNING

   The MCS500's absolute encoders can only count joint 4 over
   plus or minus ten turns — the same as the software limit. Do
   not turn the shaft by hand beyond that, for example with the
   brakes released.

   BY DEFAULT

       SetAutoConfTurn(1)   robot chooses the nearest (default)
       SetAutoConfTurn(0)   freeze the current turn
       SetConfTurn(ct)      demand a specific revolution
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Seeing the ambiguity directly -----------------------------
   These two joint sets are the same robot POSTURE. The shaft
   ends up pointing the same way both times. But getting from one
   to the other is a full revolution of the shaft — which you can
   watch happen, if you put tape on it.

   Note that we use MoveJoints here, because joint positions are
   the only way to state "the same orientation, one turn further
   on" unambiguously. */
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 30)
Delay(1.5)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 390)            // 30 + 360: same orientation
Delay(1.5)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 30)             // and back
Delay(1.5)


/* --- Controlling it from a Cartesian pose ----------------------
   Now the same idea through MovePose, where you specify an
   orientation and the robot decides which revolution to use. */

MovePose(170, 0, 80, 0)
Delay(1)

// Demand the normal range: joint 4 lands between -180 and 180.
SetConfTurn(0)
MovePose(170, 0, 80, 90)
Delay(1.5)

// Demand one turn further on. Identical pose command, identical
// tool orientation at the end — and the shaft takes a whole
// extra revolution to get there. Watch the tape, not the arm:
// the arm does not move at all.
SetConfTurn(1)
MovePose(170, 0, 80, 90)
Delay(1.5)

// Back to the normal range.
SetConfTurn(0)
MovePose(170, 0, 80, 0)


// --- Hand control back and go home ----------------------------
// For a tool with nothing connected, this is the correct state
// to leave in. Going home also unwinds joint 4 to zero, which
// the manual suggests as a good habit before powering off.
SetAutoConfTurn(1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. This one sells hardest to anyone running a cable or a tube
      to the tool. Ask whether they have ever replaced
      one that wore through. The answer is usually yes, and
      usually with feeling.

   2. Run the two SetConfTurn poses back to back. Same command,
      same final orientation, one takes a full extra turn. Then
      point out that without SetConfTurn, which one you get is
      the robot's choice, and it can differ between cycles.

   3. A pick fed by a camera is the case to think through. Every
      part arrives at a different angle, and automatic selection
      always takes the shortest turn from wherever the shaft is
      NOW. Three parts in a row each a third of a turn further
      round, and the shaft has done a full revolution without
      ever coming back. Over a shift, those shortest turns can
      walk the shaft whole revolutions away from zero, winding
      the cable as they go. Pinning the turn stops that.

   NEXT   06_blending.mxprog — cycle time you get for free.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
